          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em associahedra
            or
            %em Stasheff polytopes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{K_n\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW+complexes"} CW complexes
            that naturally arrange themselves into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+operad"} topological operad
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolves
            the standard
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+operad"} associative operad
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity-operad"} A-infinity-operad
          %p
            These were introduced by
            %a{:href => "#Stasheff1963"} Stasheff 1963
            in order to describe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            equipped with a multiplication operation that is associative up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherent
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy"} higher homotopy
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_n
            correspond to ways in which one can bracket a product of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
          %p
            Much later, the associahedra also found use in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            as a generalization of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amplituhedron"}> amplituhedron
            ” in the discussion of certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Here is the rough idea, copied, for the moment, verbatim from Markl94
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/hep-th/pdf/9411/9411208v1.pdf#page=26"} p. 26
            (for more details see references below):
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 1
            the
            %strong associahedron
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_n
            is an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-2)
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq i \leq n-2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-i-2)
            pairs of brackets between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            independent indeterminates, with suitably defined incidence maps.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} Simplicially
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} { }
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subdivision"} subdivided
            associahedra (complete with simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operadic
            structure) can be presented efficiently in terms of an abstract
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+construction"} bar construction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}: Set/\mathbb{N} \to Set/\mathbb{N}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            which takes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+set"} graded set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the non-permutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-unital+operad"} non-unital operad
            freely generated by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m: \mathcal{O}\mathcal{O} \to \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_+
            be the graded set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X_n\}_{n \geq 0}
            that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0, 1
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            for
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 2
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mo +
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: \mathcal{O}t_+ \to t_+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{O}, t_+)
            is an (augmented) simplicial graded set (an object in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                        %mo ×
                        %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\Delta^{op} \times \mathbb{N}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi …
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi α
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi m
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mo +
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mo +
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi α
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mo +
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\ldots \mathcal{O}\mathcal{O}\mathcal{O}t_+
                  \\stackrel{\stackrel{\overset{m\mathcal{O} t_+}{\to}}{\underset{\mathcal{O}m t_+}{\to}}}{\underset{\mathcal{O}\mathcal{O}\alpha}{\to}}
                  \\mathcal{O}\mathcal{O}t_+ \stackrel{\overset{m t_+}{\to}}{\underset{\mathcal{O}\alpha}{\to}} \mathcal{O}t_+ \stackrel{\alpha}{\to} t_+.
          %p
            Intuitively, the (graded set of)
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}t_+
            consists of planar trees where each inner node has two or more incoming edges, with trees graded by number of leaves; the extreme points are binary trees [corresponding to complete binary bracketings of words], whereas other trees are barycenters of higher-dimensional faces of Stasheff polytopes. The construction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{O}, t_+)
            carries a simplicial (non-permutative non-unital) operad structure, where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
            of the simplicial set at grade (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arity"} arity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            defines the barycentric subdivision of the Stasheff polytope
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(\mathcal{O}, \mathcal{O}, t_+)
            is expressed in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} finite product logic
            and geometric realization preserves finite products, the (simplicially subdivided) associahedra form in this way the components of a topological operad.
          %h2#lodays_realization Loday’s realization
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Loday"} Jean-Louis Loday
            gave a simple formula for realizing the Stasheff polytopes as a convex hull of integer coordinates in Euclidean space
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Loday04"} (Loday 2004)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_n
            denote the set of (rooted planar) binary trees with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            leaves (and hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            internal vertices). For any binary tree
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in Y_n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell_1, \ldots, \ell_{n+1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_1, \ldots, v_n
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_i
            is the closest common ancestor of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell_i
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℓ
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell_{i+1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n
            whose
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i b_i
            of the number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
            of leaves that are left descendants of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_i
            and the number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_i
            of leaves that are right descendants of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi v
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v_i
          .un_thm
            %h6#theorem_loday Theorem (Loday)
            %p
              The convex hull of the points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ M(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n \mid t \in Y_n \}
              is a realization of the Stasheff polytope of dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi …
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                        %mo )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{(x_1, \ldots, x_n): x_1 + \ldots + x_n = \binom{n}{2}\}
          %h2#illustrations Illustrations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong
