            %g{:fill => "rgb(0%, 0%, 0%)", "fill-opacity" => "1"}
              %use{:x => "494.261487", "xlink:href" => "#a4FjenFyZwM9R98g1SfXtszSbZA=-glyph-3-2", :y => "244.812"}
              %use{:x => "497.513302", "xlink:href" => "#a4FjenFyZwM9R98g1SfXtszSbZA=-glyph-3-5", :y => "244.812"}
          %p
            One can also think of this as the top-level structure of the 4th
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriental"} oriental
            %em pentagon identity
            in the definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %ul
            %li
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_5
              is the
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dual_polyhedron"} dual polyhedron
              to the
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triaugmented_triangular_prism"} triaugmented triangular prism
          %div{:style => "text-align:center"}
            %p
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/K5associahedron.png"}/
            %p
              %br/
            %p
              (image from the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polytope_K3.svg"} Wikimedia Commons
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                One can rotate and explore Stasheff polyhedra in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://ltrujello.github.io/associahedron/"} this interactive associahedron app
            %li
              %p
                Illustrations of some polytopes, including
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi K
                          %mn 5
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_5
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://irma.math.unistra.fr/~chapoton/galerie.html"} here
            %li
              %p
                A template which can be cut out and assembled into a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_5
                can be found in Appendix B of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#ChengLauda2004"} ChengLauda2004
          %h2#relation_to_other_structures Relation to other structures
          %h3#relation_to_orientals Relation to orientals
          %p
            The above list shows that the first few Stasheff polytopes are nothing but the first few
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriental"} oriental
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            increases. The orientals are free
            %strong strict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-category"} omega-categories
            on
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
          %p
            The first place this happens is the sixth oriental: where there are three tensorator squares and six pentagons in Stasheff’s
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_5
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(5)
            are collapsed.
          %p
            It was when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            made this point to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
            that Street began to think about using associahedra to define weak
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-categories
          %h3#Categorification Categorified associahedra
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorification
            of associahedra discussed in
          %ul
            %li S. Saneblidze, R. Umble, Diagonals on the permutahedra, multiplihedra and associahedra, Homology Homotopy Appl. 6(1) (2004) 363–411.
            %li#Forcey12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Forcey"}> Stefan Forcey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quotients of the multiplihedron as categorified associahedra
              = succeed ":2" do
                %strong 10
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.hha/1251811075"} Euclid
          %h3#tamari_lattice Tamari lattice
          %p
            The associahedron is closely related to a structure known as the
            = succeed "," do
              %em Tamari lattice
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n
            can be defined as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            of all parenthesizations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            variables with the order generated by rightwards reassociation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi c
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a b)c \le a(b c)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            internal nodes (and hence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            leaves), with the order generated by rightwards tree rotation. (Note the off-by-one offset from the convention for associahedra: the Tamari lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n
            corresponds to the associahedron
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{n+1}
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            As the name suggests, the Tamari lattice also carries the structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tr%28%CF%95%C2%B3%29"} Tr(ϕ³)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amplituhedron"} amplituhedron
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmohedron"} cosmohedron
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The original articles that define associahedra (and in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            that gives
            = succeed "-topological" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(\infty)
          %ul
            %li#Stasheff1963
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"}> Jim Stasheff
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy associativity of H-spaces I
                %strong 108
                (1963) 275-312 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1993608"}> jstor:1993608
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %p
                %em> Homotopy associativity of H-spaces II
                , Trans. Amer. Math. Soc.
                %strong
                  108* (1963) 293-312 &amp;lbrack;
                  %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1993609"}> jstor:1993609
                  \&amp;rbrack;
          %p A textbook discussion (slightly modified) is in section 1.6 of the book
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Markl"}> Martin Markl
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Shnider"} Steven Shnider
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
              %em Operads in Algebra, Topology and Physics
              (
              %a{:href => "http://books.google.de/books?id=fMhZjT9lQo0C&pg=PA56&lpg=PA56&dq=Stasheff+associahedra&source=bl&ots=ZuGXjT4zbp&sig=V-taGG2LHS0msHK-PTxmUXXCvEY&hl=de#PPP1,M1"}> web
              )
          %p Loday’s original article on the Stasheff polytope is
          %ul
            %li#Loday04
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Loday"}> Jean-Louis Loday
              , Realization of the Stasheff polytope,
              %em Archiv der Mathematik
              83 (2004), 267-278. (
              %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.1007%2Fs00013-004-1026-y"}> doi
              )
          %p Further explanations and references are collected at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/featurecolumn/archive/associahedra.html"} AMS entry on associahedra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Postnikov"}> Alexander Postnikov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Permutohedra, associahedra and beyond
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0507163"} math.CO/0507163
                %a{:href => "http://math.mit.edu/~apost/papers/permutohedron.pdf"} pdf
          %p The connection to Tamari lattices as well as other developments are in
          %ul
            %li#TomariFestschrift
              Folkert Müller-Hoissen, Jean Marcel Pallo,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
              (editors),
              = succeed "," do
                %em Associahedra, Tamari Lattices, and Related Structures: Tamari Memorial Festschrift
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://books.google.fr/books?id=Y01d6g5UemQC&lpg=PP1&pg=PP1#v=onepage&q&f=false"} google books
          %p
            For a template of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_5
          %ul
            %li#ChengLauda2004
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenia+Cheng"}> Eugenia Cheng
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aaron+Lauda"} Aaron Lauda
              = succeed "," do
                %em Higher-dimensional categories: an illustrated guidebook
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://eugeniacheng.com/guidebook/"} available here
          %p For the combinatorics of associahedra, see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Viviane+Pons"}> Viviane Pons
              ,
              %em Combinatorics of the Permutahedra, Associahedra, and Friends
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12687"}> arXiv:2310.12687
              )
          %p
            For the use of associahedra in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nima+Arkani-Hamed"}> Nima Arkani-Hamed
                , Yuntao Bai, Song He &amp; Gongwang Yan,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Scattering forms and the positive geometry of kinematics, color and the worldsheet
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP05(2018)096"} doi:10.1007/JHEP05(2018)096
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.09102"} arXiv:1711.09102
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nima+Arkani-Hamed"}> Nima Arkani-Hamed
                , Song He, Giulio Salvatori, Hugh Thomas,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Causal diamonds, cluster polytopes and scattering amplitudes
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => " https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP11(2022)049"} doi:10.1007/JHEP11(2022)049
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.12948"} arXiv:1912.12948
            %li
              %p
                Kristian Ranestad, Bernd Sturmfels, Simon Telen:
                %em What is Positive Geometry?
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12815"}> arXiv:2502.12815
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Ross Glew, Tomasz Lukowski,
                %em Amplitubes: Graph Cosmohedra
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.17564"}> arXiv:2502.17564
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Some discussion about the use of associahedra in physics occurred on the category theory Zulip
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://categorytheory.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/411259-theory.3A-science/topic/Amplituhedron.2C.20associahedron.2C.20and.20related.20approaches"} here
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/combinatorics"} combinatorics
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Stasheff"} Jim Stasheff
                  comments on this in an essay titled “How I ‘met’ Dov Tamari”
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#TomariFestschrift"} (Tamari Memorial Festschrift 2012)
